# Measuring a policy against what never happened
subtitle: "How difference-in-differences borrows the world without a policy from a group it missed, and the parallel-trends bet it rests on."
tags: ["economics","causal inference","difference-in-differences","policy evaluation","counterfactuals","parallel trends"]
teaches: ["concepts/difference-in-differences","concepts/parallel-trends-assumption","methods/causal-identification","concepts/counterfactual-reasoning"]
thumb: "card purple"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Orus | a firm, patient econometrics teacher; plain and confident","take":"lines"}

## Measuring a policy against what never happened
use: did_trends
with:
    effect: 1.54
    treat_at: 5
    group_labels:
      - With scheme
      - Without
    y_label: Hourly pay, $
    show_counterfactual: true
sources:
    - id: snow1855
      title: On the Mode of Communication of Cholera (2nd ed.)
      authors: John Snow
      year: 1855
      finding: Cholera deaths fell among customers of the Lambeth company after it moved its intake upstream in 1852, but not among those of Southwark and Vauxhall.

    math naive tex "22 - 18 = 4" x 300 y 600 hidden true
    math calc tex "(22 - 18) - {(17.5 - 16)} = 2.5" x 300 y 535 hidden true
    mark strike_naive around naive kind strike hidden true
    path alt smooth dashed thick #8a5fa8 points [[471,375.6],[541.2,389.8],[611.3,415.6],[681.5,451.6],[751.7,473.4],[821.9,488.6]] hidden true
    portrait portrait_john_snow cites ["snow1855"] x 640 y 610 width 120 of "John Snow" cameo true hidden true
    text v18 "$18" #b5482e x 430 y 405 font_size 26 hidden true
    text v22 "$22" #b5482e x 765 y 585 font_size 26 hidden true
    text v16 "$16" #2f6b8f x 430 y 250 font_size 26 hidden true
    text v175 "$17.50" #2f6b8f x 765 y 318 font_size 26 hidden true
    text v195 "$19.50" #8f887c x 765 y 408 font_size 26 hidden true
    text v205 "$20.50" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 505 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title Difference-in-differences

How do you measure what a policy did, when you never see the world without it? Difference-in-differences borrows that world from a group the policy missed.
    draw axes treat_line

Say one region starts an apprenticeship scheme in 2020. Young workers' pay there goes from $18 an hour to $22 by 2025.
    draw treated_line v18 v22

Before and after, the scheme seems worth $4 an hour.
    draw naive

But pay rose everywhere. In a similar region without the scheme it went from $16 to $17.50, with no scheme at all.
    draw control_line v16 v175

Before 2020 the two lines run parallel. The scheme region was $2 ahead, but the two moved in step.
    highlight treated_line control_line

The key assumption is that they would have kept moving in step: without the scheme, pay there would also have risen $1.50, to $19.50.
    draw counterfactual v195

That dashed path is never observed. It is borrowed from the other region.
    highlight counterfactual

The effect is the gap between what happened and that path: $22 minus $19.50.
    draw effect_arrow

Two differences: the change with the scheme, minus the change without it. $2.50 an hour.
    draw calc

Not $4: before-and-after handed the scheme a rise that everyone got.
    draw strike_naive

Subtracting changes, not levels, removes the $2 head start, which never moved, and the $1.50 rise both regions got anyway.
    highlight calc part "(17.5 - 16)"

But suppose the scheme region had been pulling ahead anyway, and would have reached $20.50. Then the effect is only $1.50.
    draw alt v205

Nothing after 2020 can tell those two paths apart. Only the years before can make parallel trends believable.
    erase alt v205

John Snow used the idea in 1850s London: cholera deaths among customers of a water company that moved its intake upstream, against a company that did not.
    draw portrait_john_snow

    erase portrait_john_snow

So the groups need not be alike. They need to be groups that would have changed alike.
    point at.ref effect_arrow gesture circle

    quiz "Before the policy, pay in the treated region was $5 higher than in the control. Does that break difference-in-differences?"
        right: No. A steady gap is subtracted away with the first differences; what breaks the method is a gap that would have changed without the policy.
        * Yes: the groups must start at the same level
        + No: only their trends must match
        * Only if the gap is larger than the effect
---
